## Reasoning

**Entity types in the schema.** The stream is a MarineTraffic AIS feed. Reading the columns, I see: (1) a ship, identified by several identifier schemes (`mmsi`, `imo`, `ship_id`, `callsign`); (2) a timestamped observation of that ship (`timestamp`, `dsrc`); (3) a position (`lat`, `lon`); (4) a kinematic state (`speed`, `course`) and a separate orientation (`heading`); (5) flag/country affiliation (`flag`); (6) three ports referenced per row (`current_port*`, `last_port*`, `next_port*`), each with a name, an internal id, a UN/LOCODE and a country.

**Subjects.** The ship's subject must come from a column populated in every record: `mmsi` is present in all three samples and is the AIS unique identifier, so `vessel_{mmsi}`. `imo` and `ship_id` would also work but `mmsi` is the feed's primary key. The class is `bsm:Vessel` (`type_name` = Frigate/Submarine/Corvette, `shiptype` = 35). Observation/location/velocity/orientation subjects must vary per stream tick, so they are keyed on `{mmsi}_{timestamp}`; keying them only on `mmsi` would merge every tick of the same ship into one state.

**Object properties leading out of Vessel.** From `bsm:Vehicle` there is `bsm:hasObservation` → `bsm:Observation` (`observationDateTime` ← `timestamp`). From `bsm:BattlespaceConcept` there is `bsm:hasGeographicLocation` → `bsm:Location`, and `bsm:Location` has `bsm:hasGeometry` → `bsm:AbsolutePoint` carrying `latitudeDecimal`/`longitudeDecimal` — so `lat`/`lon` must not be flattened as literals on the vessel. The unattached `bsm:hasVelocity` / `bsm:hasOrientation` give `bsm:Velocity` (`speedMetersPerSecond`, `absoluteBearingAngle`) and `bsm:Orientation` (`headingAngle`). From `bsm:Object` there is `bsm:hasAffiliation` → `bsm:GeopoliticalAffiliation` for `flag`. From `bsm:BattlespaceConcept` there is `bsm:hasAlternateIdentifier` → `bsm:AlternateIdentifier`.

**The AlternateIdentifier check.** This is exactly the "one generic value plus its scheme" shape I should normally avoid — but here the schema genuinely shows *several distinct naming schemes for the same ship at once* (`imo`, `ship_id`, `callsign`, on top of the `mmsi` used as subject), and for ports both an internal `*_port_id` and a `*_port_unlocode`. That is the structure the class models, so it is warranted, with the standard code supplied as a constant per scheme.

**Ports.** Ports are strongly evidenced (`last_port` = GDYNIA/KLAIPEDA, `last_port_unlocode` = PLGDY/LTKLJ, countries PL/LT), so they get their own `bsm:Port` subjects. I key them on the UN/LOCODE, not on `*_port_id`: the ids differ per row (1200/1201/1202) for what is the same physical port GDYNIA, which would wrongly split it, while the locode merges correctly. I found no object property anywhere in the ontology linking a `bsm:Vessel`/`bsm:Materiel` to a `bsm:Facility` (only `bsm:hasControlledFacility`, whose domain is `bsm:Actor`), so the ports stand as their own subjects rather than being forced onto an invented relation.

**Near-synonym decisions.** `speed` vs `max_speed`: `bsm:vesselMaximumSpeed` carries the qualifier "maximum", so it takes `max_speed` only; the instantaneous `speed` goes to `bsm:speedMetersPerSecond` — note the unit qualifier disagrees (AIS speed 84 is tenths of knots, not m/s) and no arithmetic is permitted, so I flag this as a unit-ambiguous mapping with reduced confidence. `heading` vs `course`: `bsm:headingAngle` on Orientation is the hull heading; `bsm:absoluteBearingAngle` on Velocity is the course over ground — the two AIS columns split cleanly along that qualifier. `flag` → `bsm:geopoliticalAffiliationCode` rather than `bsm:hostilityCode`: a flag state is registry, not hostility. `bsm:conceptName` vs `bsm:alternateName` vs `bsm:locationName`: `shipname`/port names are primary names, and Port is a Facility (an Object), not a `bsm:Location`, so `conceptName` is correct in both cases. `ship_class` → `bsm:modelName` is plausible (vessel class ≈ model) but the column is empty in all samples, so it is low-confidence and will simply be skipped per record.

**Unmapped.** `utc_seconds`, `market`, `ais_type_summary` (no military/civilian code exists outside Aircraft), all hull dimensions (`length`, `width`, `l_fore`, `w_left`) and tonnages (`grt`, `dwt`, `draught`) — the only dimension property, `wingSpanDimension`, is Aircraft-only; `year_built`, `ship_country` (only the code form is modelled, via `flag`), `rot`, `destination`, `eta`, `eta_calc`, `eta_updated`, `last_port_time`, `distance_to_go`, `distance_travelled`, `avg_speed`.

**IRI transformation re-check.** `vessel_{mmsi}` (subject only); `observation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}`, `location_{mmsi}_{timestamp}`, `point_{mmsi}_{timestamp}`, `velocity_{mmsi}_{timestamp}`, `orientation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}`, `affiliation_{flag}`, `altid_imo_{imo}`, `altid_callsign_{callsign}`, `altid_mtid_{ship_id}`, `port_{last_port_unlocode}`, `port_{next_port_unlocode}`, `port_{current_port_unlocode}`, `altid_port_{last_port_id}`, `altid_unlocode_{last_port_unlocode}`, and the corresponding next/current variants — each string appears in the referencing value exactly as in its subject.

